1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:07,650
Hi, I'm Shivam Gupta, and this is Looplight.

2
00:00:08,350 --> 00:00:13,804
Anita has just left the hospital. Her daughter
Maya has the discharge document.

3
00:00:13,804 --> 00:00:20,096
But a result is still pending, another test needs
follow-up, and someone needs to track

4
00:00:20,096 --> 00:00:23,452
what happens next. Anita and Maya are fictional.

5
00:00:23,452 --> 00:00:25,550
The coordination problem is real.

6
00:00:26,250 --> 00:00:31,293
Looplight turns the instructions in a
discharge document into a follow-up ledger.

7
00:00:31,293 --> 00:00:35,915
Every confirmed action keeps its source,
the person tracking it, and

8
00:00:35,915 --> 00:00:38,017
a record of what happened.

9
00:00:38,717 --> 00:00:44,166
Let's follow Anita's example. I open the
sample document and run the analysis.

10
00:00:44,166 --> 00:00:49,616
A small trained text model works with
explicit rules to suggest follow-up items.

11
00:00:49,616 --> 00:00:52,550
It runs without a paid model API.

12
00:00:53,250 --> 00:00:56,184
The review screen is the important part.

13
00:00:56,184 --> 00:00:59,957
I can see the exact sentence behind each
suggestion.

14
00:00:59,957 --> 00:01:05,825
I review the whole document, including sentences
the system did not turn into tasks.

15
00:01:05,825 --> 00:01:10,017
I can correct a suggestion or add something it
missed.

16
00:01:10,717 --> 00:01:13,664
Dates remain connected to the original
wording.

17
00:01:13,664 --> 00:01:19,136
If the document is unclear, Looplight
leaves a question for the care team.

18
00:01:19,136 --> 00:01:22,083
It does not invent a clinical deadline.

19
00:01:22,783 --> 00:01:27,832
I confirm the follow-up and give Maya
responsibility for tracking one action.

20
00:01:27,832 --> 00:01:32,880
That records who is following up. It does
not mean Maya is

21
00:01:32,880 --> 00:01:34,983
the clinician responsible for treatment.

22
00:01:35,683 --> 00:01:40,732
Now a result has arrived. Watch what
happens when I record that.

23
00:01:40,732 --> 00:01:44,939
The result can be received while clinician
review remains outstanding.

24
00:01:44,939 --> 00:01:47,883
A file arriving is only one step.

25
00:01:48,583 --> 00:01:54,049
I record the separate report of clinician
review and the outcome before closing

26
00:01:54,049 --> 00:01:59,515
the loop. The history shows what the user
reported, rather than presenting it

27
00:01:59,515 --> 00:02:01,617
as independently verified medical evidence.

28
00:02:02,317 --> 00:02:09,037
The plan can also leave the app. I can
print a brief for the next conversation,

29
00:02:09,037 --> 00:02:12,397
export calendar reminders, or download the
structured record.

30
00:02:12,397 --> 00:02:17,017
In a signed-in care space, plans are saved
to the account.

31
00:02:17,717 --> 00:02:23,585
For the first customer, I would test this with a
primary-care or transitions coordinator

32
00:02:23,585 --> 00:02:29,034
who already chases discharge follow-up. The
initial pricing hypothesis is one hundred and

33
00:02:29,034 --> 00:02:32,806
forty-nine dollars per month for one hundred care
episodes.

34
00:02:32,806 --> 00:02:37,417
The pilot must show whether staff time saved
justifies that price.

35
00:02:38,117 --> 00:02:43,569
This is a working MVP with synthetic evaluation,
not a clinically validated product.

36
00:02:43,569 --> 00:02:48,603
The model's raw five-category accuracy was
eighty-two percent on fifty separate synthetic

37
00:02:48,603 --> 00:02:53,217
challenge examples. It abstained on forty-four,
so human review remains central.

38
00:02:53,917 --> 00:02:59,748
The next step is a supervised pilot that
measures omissions and preparation time.

39
00:02:59,748 --> 00:03:02,440
I built Looplight with AI-assisted
development.

40
00:03:02,440 --> 00:03:07,374
The goal is simple: every next step has a
source, a

41
00:03:07,374 --> 00:03:09,617
person, and a recorded ending.

